WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.331
Storytelling.

2
00:00:01.611 --> 00:00:06.155
Day 21 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Communication phase.

3
00:00:07.905 --> 00:00:08.929
The key idea.

4
00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:17.657
Today's goal: turn facts into short stories people remember, for interviews, presentations, team updates and everyday conversation.

5
00:00:19.007 --> 00:00:19.861
Part 1.

6
00:00:20.141 --> 00:00:21.442
Why stories work.

7
00:00:22.692 --> 00:00:28.559
People forget bullet points but remember stories: a person, a problem, a struggle and a result.

8
00:00:28.839 --> 00:00:34.236
A 30-second story makes your point stick in an interview, a demo, or a request for budget.

9
00:00:35.586 --> 00:00:36.461
Part 2.

10
00:00:36.741 --> 00:00:38.085
The simple story arc.

11
00:00:39.335 --> 00:00:40.252
Step 1:

12
00:00:40.532 --> 00:00:43.028
Setting: when, where, who (Last Diwali sale...).

13
00:00:43.308 --> 00:00:44.225
Step 2:

14
00:00:44.505 --> 00:00:46.831
Problem: what went wrong or what was at stake.

15
00:00:47.111 --> 00:00:48.028
Step 3:

16
00:00:48.308 --> 00:00:51.039
Struggle: what you tried; tension and choices.

17
00:00:51.319 --> 00:00:52.279
Step 4:

18
00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:54.991
Turning point: the key action or insight.

19
00:00:55.271 --> 00:00:56.231
Step 5:

20
00:00:56.511 --> 00:01:00.116
Result and lesson: what changed, with a number, and what you learned.

21
00:01:01.466 --> 00:01:02.341
Example:

22
00:01:02.621 --> 00:01:03.986
From facts to story.

23
00:01:04.266 --> 00:01:05.055
Facts:

24
00:01:05.335 --> 00:01:06.957
We added canary deploys.

25
00:01:07.237 --> 00:01:08.538
Outages went down.

26
00:01:08.818 --> 00:01:09.629
Story:

27
00:01:09.909 --> 00:01:15.903
Last Diwali, our biggest sales day, a deploy at 7 pm took checkout down for 40 minutes.

28
00:01:16.183 --> 00:01:18.743
We lost about 1,200 orders.

29
00:01:19.023 --> 00:01:23.055
The next morning, the team was exhausted and the CEO wanted answers.

30
00:01:23.335 --> 00:01:27.815
We realised the problem wasn't the code; it was releasing to everyone at once.

31
00:01:28.095 --> 00:01:31.061
So we started releasing to 5% of users first.

32
00:01:31.341 --> 00:01:35.245
This Diwali, we deployed eleven times and had zero outages.

33
00:01:35.525 --> 00:01:38.021
Small, careful steps beat big brave ones.

34
00:01:39.371 --> 00:01:40.267
Part 3.

35
00:01:40.547 --> 00:01:41.592
Story tools.

36
00:01:42.842 --> 00:01:44.186
Here is a quick table:

37
00:01:44.466 --> 00:01:45.703
Tool, and Example.

38
00:01:45.983 --> 00:01:47.050
Start in the middle:

39
00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:49.805
At 7:10 pm, the alerts started.

40
00:01:50.085 --> 00:01:53.605
Use a real detail: ...the dashboard turned completely red.

41
00:01:53.885 --> 00:01:54.994
Include dialogue:

42
00:01:55.274 --> 00:01:57.941
My manager asked, 'How long until it's back?'.

43
00:01:58.221 --> 00:01:59.245
Use numbers:

44
00:01:59.525 --> 00:02:03.450
1,200 orders, 40 minutes, zero outages.

45
00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:05.031
End with one lesson:

46
00:02:05.311 --> 00:02:07.807
Small, careful steps beat big brave ones.

47
00:02:08.087 --> 00:02:09.090
Keep it short:

48
00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:11.887
30 to 90 seconds for most work stories.

49
00:02:13.237 --> 00:02:14.155
Part 4.

50
00:02:14.435 --> 00:02:15.672
Where to use stories.

51
00:02:16.922 --> 00:02:18.266
Here is a quick table:

52
00:02:18.546 --> 00:02:20.423
Situation, and Story type.

53
00:02:20.703 --> 00:02:21.535
Interview:

54
00:02:21.815 --> 00:02:22.946
A STAR story:

55
00:02:23.226 --> 00:02:26.405
Situation, Task, Action, Result (see Interviews).

56
00:02:26.685 --> 00:02:27.751
Presentation:

57
00:02:28.031 --> 00:02:31.146
Open with a short story instead of an agenda slide.

58
00:02:31.426 --> 00:02:32.706
Asking for budget:

59
00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:36.079
The customer or team pain, in one real example.

60
00:02:36.359 --> 00:02:37.362
Team update:

61
00:02:37.642 --> 00:02:40.095
Here's what happened with one customer this week...

62
00:02:40.375 --> 00:02:41.378
Small talk:

63
00:02:41.658 --> 00:02:44.410
A funny or surprising travel or weekend story.

64
00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:46.720
Part 5.

65
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.195
Useful phrases.

66
00:02:49.445 --> 00:02:51.109
Let me tell you about the time we...

67
00:02:51.389 --> 00:02:52.711
It all started when...

68
00:02:52.991 --> 00:02:55.487
At first, we thought... but then we realised...

69
00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:57.239
That's when we decided to...

70
00:02:57.519 --> 00:03:00.207
In the end... / Looking back, the lesson was...

71
00:03:01.557 --> 00:03:02.496
Part 6.

72
00:03:02.776 --> 00:03:05.357
Conversation: telling a story in an interview.

73
00:03:06.607 --> 00:03:08.634
Here is how the conversation goes.

74
00:03:08.914 --> 00:03:11.026
Tell me about a time you made a mistake.

75
00:03:11.306 --> 00:03:12.031
Sure.

76
00:03:12.311 --> 00:03:19.885
Two years ago, I ran a database migration on a Friday evening without testing it on a copy of production data.

77
00:03:20.165 --> 00:03:21.210
What happened?

78
00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:24.093
It locked the orders table for twelve minutes.

79
00:03:24.373 --> 00:03:28.085
Customers couldn't place orders, and my phone started ringing.

80
00:03:29.435 --> 00:03:31.035
The conversation continues.

81
00:03:31.315 --> 00:03:32.723
How did you handle it?

82
00:03:33.003 --> 00:03:37.440
I rolled it back first, then told my manager exactly what I'd done.

83
00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:44.376
The next week, I added a rule to CI: every migration runs on a production snapshot first.

84
00:03:44.656 --> 00:03:45.787
And the result?

85
00:03:46.067 --> 00:03:48.819
We haven't had a migration outage since then.

86
00:03:49.099 --> 00:03:54.645
The lesson for me was: own the mistake quickly, then fix the system, not just the bug.

87
00:03:55.995 --> 00:03:56.934
Part 7.

88
00:03:57.214 --> 00:03:58.131
Practice.

89
00:03:59.381 --> 00:04:04.224
Write a 60-second story about a problem you solved at work, using the 5-step arc.

90
00:04:04.504 --> 00:04:05.997
Tell it aloud and time it.

91
00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:08.222
Part 8.

92
00:04:08.502 --> 00:04:09.547
Speak today.

93
00:04:10.797 --> 00:04:14.979
Prepare one work story and one personal story you can tell in under a minute.

94
00:04:15.259 --> 00:04:19.760
Start your next update or presentation with a short story instead of an agenda.

95
00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:22.646
That's the end of this lesson.

96
00:04:22.926 --> 00:04:27.577
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

97
00:04:27.857 --> 00:04:33.979
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

